import Foundation

enum SCPlanTableD {
    static let plans: [SCPlan] = [

        scplan("marriage",
               "Marriage",
               "Fourteen days on staying kind",
               .love,
               "Fourteen days on the ordinary work of loving another person over time: patience, honesty, forgiveness and attention. It makes no assumptions about how your own marriage is going. Read it married, read it while things are hard, read it on your own.",
               [
                scday("Not alone",
                      ["Genesis 2:18", "Ecclesiastes 4:9-10"],
                      "The first thing called not good in the whole account is a person left by himself. Ecclesiastes says it plainly: two can lift what one alone cannot, and if one falls the other reaches down. That is a modest claim. It says nothing about romance and a great deal about presence. Married or not, whatever shape your household has taken, think of who has reached down for you recently, and whether you thanked them."),

                scday("What charity means",
                      ["1 Corinthians 13:4-5", "Colossians 3:12-13"],
                      "Charity in the older English simply means love, and Paul describes it almost entirely by what it does not do. It is not easily provoked. It does not keep an account of wrongs. Notice how much of it is restraint rather than feeling. Colossians adds the same in different clothing: kindness, meekness, longsuffering. None of that requires a good day. It only requires not doing the sharp thing you could do."),

                scday("Slow to anger",
                      ["James 1:19-20", "Proverbs 14:29"],
                      "Swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath. James puts the order deliberately, and most of us reverse it. Proverbs adds that the person slow to anger has great understanding, as if patience were a kind of intelligence rather than a temperament. Anger arrives fast because it is certain. Understanding arrives slowly because it is asking questions. Today, when something stings, try letting one full breath pass before you answer."),

                scday("A soft answer",
                      ["Proverbs 15:1", "Proverbs 10:19"],
                      "A soft answer turns away wrath. It is an odd piece of physics, but anyone who has lived beside another person knows it is true, and knows how hard it is to do while the blood is up. The second proverb goes further: in the multitude of words there is no shortage of trouble. Not every thought needs saying tonight. Some of them are true and still not worth what they cost."),

                scday("Bearing all things",
                      ["1 Corinthians 13:6-7", "1 Peter 4:8"],
                      "Beareth, believeth, hopeth, endureth. Four verbs with no object named, because the point is that love keeps going when the object is unimpressive. Peter says charity covers a multitude of sins, which is not pretending they are absent but declining to display them. There is a difference between covering and hiding. Covering is what you do with a coat over someone shivering. It costs the one who does it."),

                scday("Seventy times seven",
                      ["Matthew 18:21-22", "Ephesians 4:31-32"],
                      "Peter thought seven forgivenesses was generous. The answer he got makes counting itself the mistake. Ephesians describes the same move as putting away bitterness and clamour, then adds the reason: forgive as you have been forgiven. Forgiving does not mean the harm was small, and it does not oblige anyone to stand where they are being harmed. It means refusing to carry the ledger any further than today."),

                scday("Words that build",
                      ["Ephesians 4:29-30", "Proverbs 25:11"],
                      "Corrupt communication in the older sense means rotten, the way fruit goes. Paul's replacement is speech that builds and that ministers grace to the hearer. Proverbs compares a word fitly spoken to apples of gold in settings of silver, which is a picture of timing as much as content. The same sentence lands differently at breakfast than at midnight. What would it take to say the true thing at a better hour?"),

                scday("Search me",
                      ["Psalms 139:23-24", "Proverbs 28:13"],
                      "This is a strange prayer to pray on purpose: search me, and know my thoughts. Most of us would rather not be searched. But the psalm asks for it, and Proverbs adds that covering our faults gets us nowhere while confessing them opens something. In a shared life the hardest honesty is usually with yourself first. Before deciding what the other person needs to admit, sit for a moment with your own part."),

                scday("Attention",
                      ["Proverbs 12:25", "Philippians 2:3-4"],
                      "Heaviness in the heart makes it stoop, says the proverb, but a good word makes it glad. That is attention doing its work, and it requires noticing that someone is stooping in the first place. Paul asks us to look on the things of others, not only our own. Nothing dramatic is prescribed. Just the discipline of lifting your eyes from your own concerns long enough to see what today has cost the person beside you."),

                scday("Passing over",
                      ["Proverbs 19:11", "Proverbs 10:12"],
                      "It is his glory to pass over a transgression. Not to miss it, not to excuse it, but to let it go by unremarked. Proverbs elsewhere says hatred stirs up strife while love covers everything. Some grievances need saying out loud. Many do not, and the skill of a long shared life is telling them apart. Which of the small things you are keeping this week could simply be allowed to pass?"),

                scday("A threefold cord",
                      ["Ecclesiastes 4:12", "Romans 12:9-10"],
                      "A cord of three strands is not quickly broken. The image is practical rather than mystical: strength comes from more than one strand, and rope frays slowly rather than snapping. Paul asks for love without dissimulation, which means without acting, and for a preference toward honouring the other person first. Two people held together by nothing but feeling are a single strand. What else is braided into yours?"),

                scday("Laying down",
                      ["John 15:12-13", "1 John 3:16-18"],
                      "Greater love hath no man than this. It sounds like a sentence about dying, and it is, but John immediately makes it about a coat and a meal: if you have goods and see someone in need, love is not in word or in tongue but in deed. Laying down a life mostly happens in instalments. An early morning. A held tongue. An errand nobody thanked you for."),

                scday("Kindness as fruit",
                      ["Galatians 5:22-23", "1 Peter 3:8-9"],
                      "Fruit is not manufactured. It grows out of what the tree is connected to, slowly and mostly out of sight, and gentleness appears on the list right alongside patience and self-control. Peter's version is a household one: be pitiful, in the old sense of full of pity, and courteous, and do not return railing for railing. That last part is the whole difficulty. Somebody has to absorb the blow rather than pass it on."),

                scday("What remains",
                      ["1 Corinthians 13:8-10", "1 Corinthians 13:13"],
                      "Knowledge will vanish away, Paul says, and prophecy will fail, and we currently know in part. Very little of what we are certain about today will survive. What lasts is faith, hope and love, and the greatest of these is the plainest. That is a quiet comfort for anyone whose shared life has not gone as planned. The part of it that was love is the part that does not expire."),
               ]),

        scplan("parenting",
               "Parenting",
               "Fourteen days for tired parents",
               .love,
               "Fourteen short readings for anyone raising a child, at any stage and in any arrangement. It offers no technique and makes no promises about how children turn out. It is mostly about seeing them clearly, and about being kind to yourself on the days that go badly.",
               [
                scday("Let them come",
                      ["Mark 10:14-16", "Matthew 19:14"],
                      "The disciples were managing the schedule, and children were not on it. The interruption is welcomed instead, and the children are held rather than merely permitted. Whatever else you take from the scene, notice that it happens in the middle of a working day, and that the adults present had good reasons for their impatience. Most days a child's request arrives at exactly the wrong moment. It is still a request."),

                scday("Known first",
                      ["Psalms 139:14", "Jeremiah 1:5"],
                      "Fearfully and wonderfully made is a strange, strong phrase, and the psalm applies it to a body being knit together out of sight. Jeremiah hears something similar: known before formed. Neither verse says anything about achievement. They locate a person's worth somewhere earlier than anything they will manage to do. That is worth remembering on report card day, and on the days when you are quietly comparing your child to somebody else's."),

                scday("Who builds the house",
                      ["Psalms 127:1", "Proverbs 3:5-6"],
                      "Except the Lord build the house, they labour in vain that build it. Read one way that is discouraging. Read another it is a release: the outcome was never entirely yours to engineer. Proverbs asks for trust rather than leaning on your own understanding, which is not a ban on thinking but a limit on certainty. You will still do the work. You are simply not the only one at it."),

                scday("Wisdom you lack",
                      ["James 1:5-8", "Proverbs 2:6"],
                      "If any of you lack wisdom, let him ask. James assumes the lack rather than treating it as a failure, and adds that the asking is met without upbraiding, meaning without being scolded for needing help. Nobody arrives at this equipped. The specific question you have tonight about your child is probably not in any book. Ask it plainly, and then ask a wiser friend as well."),

                scday("Like a father",
                      ["Psalms 103:13", "Isaiah 49:15"],
                      "Like as a father pitieth his children. Pity in the older sense is tenderness, not condescension, and the psalm assumes the reader knows the feeling from the inside. Isaiah reaches for a mother who cannot forget her nursing child, then says even that can fail while something else will not. These are pictures of God drawn from parents, which means the ordinary ache you feel is being used as evidence."),

                scday("Carried",
                      ["Isaiah 40:11", "Isaiah 66:13"],
                      "He shall gather the lambs with his arm, and gently lead those that are with young. That last phrase is about the ones doing the carrying, who cannot move fast because someone small depends on them. If you are being led gently rather than driven, that is the point. Isaiah later says comfort comes as a mother comforts. Even the people who do the comforting need comforting sometimes."),

                scday("Slow to speak",
                      ["James 1:19-20", "Proverbs 15:1"],
                      "Swift to hear, slow to speak, slow to wrath. In a house with children the first is the hardest, because there is a great deal of noise and most of it seems to need an immediate ruling. James says the wrath of man does not produce the good it promises. A soft answer, Proverbs adds, turns wrath aside. Not a soft rule. A soft voice saying whatever the rule already was."),

                scday("Not as man sees",
                      ["1 Samuel 16:7", "Genesis 1:27"],
                      "Man looks on the outward appearance. It is a warning aimed at a prophet doing an important job, and it applies uncomfortably well to how we size up our own children: the loud one, the quiet one, the difficult one. Genesis says the image is there before any of that. Try describing your child today without a single word about performance, behavior or comparison. It is harder than it sounds."),

                scday("Sparrows counted",
                      ["Luke 12:6-7", "Matthew 10:29-31"],
                      "Five sparrows sold for two farthings, and not one forgotten. The arithmetic is deliberately unimpressive; sparrows were the cheapest thing at market. Then the hairs of your head are numbered. The claim is not that nothing bad happens to sparrows, because plainly it does. The claim is about attention, that nothing here goes unnoticed. Say it over your child tonight, and then over yourself."),

                scday("Learning from them",
                      ["Matthew 18:3-4", "Psalms 8:3-4"],
                      "Except ye become as little children. The adults were arguing about rank when a child was set in the middle of them as the answer, which must have been humiliating and was meant to be. The psalm does something similar with a night sky: what is man, that thou art mindful of him. Children are good at both wonder and dependence. Most of us spent years unlearning those. Watch how yours does it."),

                scday("Kept things",
                      ["Luke 2:19", "Ecclesiastes 3:1"],
                      "Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart. She did not explain them or post them anywhere. She kept them, which is a quieter kind of attention and available to anyone. Ecclesiastes adds that there is a season to everything, and the season you are in now with your child will not be the one you are in next year. Keep something from this week before it goes."),

                scday("On the road",
                      ["Luke 15:20-21", "Proverbs 4:23"],
                      "When he was yet a great way off, his father saw him. Which means the father had been watching that road for a long time. The son had his apology rehearsed and never got to finish it. Whatever your child is doing or not doing, the picture here is of waiting that does not curdle into contempt. Keep your heart, says Proverbs, with all diligence, because everything else flows out of it."),

                scday("When you are empty",
                      ["Matthew 11:28-30", "2 Corinthians 12:9-10"],
                      "Come unto me, all ye that labour and are heavy laden. Parenting produces a particular sort of tiredness that sleep does not entirely fix. The yoke offered is described as easy, which in the older sense means well fitted rather than light. Paul goes further and says strength turns up inside weakness. You do not have to be doing this well today for it to be worth doing today."),

                scday("Walking in truth",
                      ["3 John 1:4", "Deuteronomy 6:4-5"],
                      "I have no greater joy than to hear that my children walk in truth. John is writing about people he mentored rather than his own household, which widens the word considerably. Deuteronomy pictures teaching that happens sitting down, walking, lying down and rising up, which is to say in no special place at all. Most of what a child absorbs from you was never a lesson. It was a Tuesday."),
               ]),

        scplan("work-and-calling",
               "Work and Calling",
               "Ten days on ordinary work",
               .guidance,
               "Most of a life is spent working, and most of that work is unremarkable. Ten days on what the text actually says about labour, diligence, plans that change and the quiet question of what a person is for. Useful whether you love your job, tolerate it, or are looking for one.",
               [
                scday("With your hands",
                      ["Colossians 3:23-24", "Ecclesiastes 9:10"],
                      "Whatsoever ye do, do it heartily. The instruction is given to servants doing work nobody admired, which makes it harder to read as advice for people with interesting careers. Ecclesiastes is blunter still: whatever your hand finds to do, do it with your might. Neither passage promises the work will be recognized. Both assume it is being seen anyway. Something in that is either a comfort or a challenge, depending on your week."),

                scday("Commit the work",
                      ["Proverbs 16:3", "Proverbs 16:9"],
                      "Commit thy works, and thy thoughts shall be established. Note the order. The committing comes first and the settled mind comes after, not before. A few verses later: a man devises his way, but his steps are directed. That is not a claim that planning is pointless. It is a claim that planning is partial. Make the plan. Hold it in an open hand, and see what the week does with it."),

                scday("Vanity and vapour",
                      ["Ecclesiastes 1:2", "Ecclesiastes 3:11"],
                      "Vanity of vanities. The word behind it means vapour or breath, something real that will not stay still long enough to hold. Anyone who has watched a project they built get reorganised out of existence knows the feeling. The same book says he has set eternity in the human heart, which is why the vapour bothers us. We were not built to be satisfied by output alone."),

                scday("Unless it is built",
                      ["Psalms 127:1", "Psalms 90:12"],
                      "Except the Lord build the house, they labour in vain that build it. The psalm continues into the small hours, the bread of sorrows, the sleeplessness of people carrying too much. Then Psalm 90 asks for the ability to number our days. Not to work less, necessarily, but to know how many there are. Consider what your current pace assumes about the length of your life."),

                scday("Ordered steps",
                      ["Psalms 37:23", "Proverbs 19:21"],
                      "The steps of a good man are ordered. A step is a small unit; the psalm does not say the whole route is shown in advance. Proverbs adds that there are many plans in a person's heart but one purpose that stands. Careers rarely proceed the way the plan said. If you are between things, or in something you did not choose, this is a reminder that a step is still a step."),

                scday("Go to the ant",
                      ["Proverbs 6:6", "Galatians 6:7-9"],
                      "Go to the ant, thou sluggard. It is a rude opening, and the lesson is unglamorous: the ant works without a supervisor. Paul makes the same observation about sowing and reaping, then adds the harder part, that we should not be weary in well doing because the harvest comes in due season and not on our schedule. Most useful work is invisible for a long time before it is not."),

                scday("Whatever you do",
                      ["1 Corinthians 10:31", "1 Corinthians 15:57-58"],
                      "Whether therefore ye eat or drink, do all to the glory of God. Eating and drinking are the examples chosen, which lowers the bar considerably from what we usually imagine a calling to be. Paul closes the resurrection chapter with the promise that labour is not in vain. Not that it will succeed. That it is not wasted. Those are different claims, and the second one survives more Mondays."),

                scday("Not weary",
                      ["2 Thessalonians 3:13", "Isaiah 40:29"],
                      "Be not weary in well doing. It is one short line, and it acknowledges that weariness is the likely outcome rather than a sign of failure. Isaiah says power is given to the faint, which means the faint are the intended recipients. If you are running on fumes at work, the text is not scolding you for it. Consider what you would need to say honestly to someone this week."),

                scday("Faithful in little",
                      ["Matthew 25:21", "Zechariah 4:10"],
                      "Thou hast been faithful over a few things. In the story the commendation is identical for the servant with five talents and the one with two, which quietly removes scale from the measure. Zechariah asks who has despised the day of small things, expecting the answer to be most of us. A small job done well is not a rehearsal for a real one. It is the thing itself."),

                scday("Tomorrow's business",
                      ["James 4:13-15", "Proverbs 27:1"],
                      "Today or tomorrow we will go into such a city and buy and sell and get gain. James is not against commerce; he is against the certainty in that sentence. Your life, he says, is a vapour. Proverbs says do not boast about tomorrow because you do not know what a day brings. Neither forbids planning. Both suggest holding the calendar a little more loosely than we tend to."),
               ]),

        scplan("money-contentment",
               "Money and Contentment",
               "Ten days on having enough",
               .provision,
               "Ten days on money, appetite and what enough might mean. There is no promise here that faith produces wealth, and no suggestion that being short of money is a verdict on anyone. Mostly it is about the restlessness that money produces regardless of how much of it there is.",
               [
                scday("Daily bread",
                      ["Matthew 6:9-13", "Exodus 16:4"],
                      "Give us this day our daily bread. Not this year's bread, and not a reserve. The manna in Exodus worked the same way, gathered a day at a time, and it spoiled when hoarded. That is an inconvenient arrangement for anyone who likes a buffer. It is also the arrangement most people are actually in, whether or not their bank balance disguises it. What would today's portion look like, honestly counted?"),

                scday("Godliness with contentment",
                      ["1 Timothy 6:6-8", "Philippians 4:11-13"],
                      "We brought nothing into this world, and it is certain we can carry nothing out. Having food and clothing, says Paul, let us be content with that. Elsewhere he adds that contentment was something he learned, in both directions: how to be abased and how to abound. It did not come naturally to him either. That is worth knowing before you judge yourself for finding it difficult."),

                scday("Never satisfied",
                      ["Ecclesiastes 5:10", "Luke 12:15"],
                      "He that loveth silver shall not be satisfied with silver. The observation is about appetite rather than morality, and anyone who has hit a number and immediately set a new one knows it holds. Luke puts it as a warning about covetousness, adding that a life does not consist of the abundance of things possessed. Try naming the amount that would be enough. Then ask whether you have named that amount before."),

                scday("Two masters",
                      ["Matthew 6:24", "Matthew 6:19-21"],
                      "Ye cannot serve God and mammon. Mammon is not evil money; it is money treated as a master, something you organise your obedience around. The preceding verses talk about moth and rust and thieves, which is a practical point about where value keeps and where it does not. Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also. The heart follows the money, not the other way round."),

                scday("The root",
                      ["1 Timothy 6:10-12", "Proverbs 22:1"],
                      "The love of money is the root of all evil. The line is often shortened by dropping the love, which changes it entirely. Money itself is not accused here; the attachment is. Proverbs sets a good name above great riches, which is not a sentimental preference but a statement about what actually lasts in a community. Both verses assume you will have to choose between them at some point."),

                scday("Ravens and lilies",
                      ["Matthew 6:25-26", "Matthew 6:28-30"],
                      "Behold the fowls of the air: they sow not, neither do they reap. It would be a cruel argument if it meant nobody goes hungry, and it does not mean that. It is about attention, not immunity. Consider the lilies, how they grow. The invitation is to look at something that is not anxious for a while, which is a small act and surprisingly hard to schedule."),

                scday("Seek first",
                      ["Matthew 6:33-34", "Luke 12:29-31"],
                      "Take therefore no thought for the morrow. Take no thought in the older English means do not be anxious, not do not plan; the same passage assumes fields and barns exist. Sufficient unto the day is the evil thereof, which is almost funny in its realism. Today already has enough in it. Borrowing from tomorrow's worry does not reduce tomorrow's supply."),

                scday("Two mites",
                      ["Mark 12:43-44", "2 Corinthians 9:6-7"],
                      "She of her want did cast in all that she had. The sum was negligible and the notice taken of it was not. Whatever else is going on in that scene, the measure is proportion rather than size. Paul says the giver should not give grudgingly or of necessity, because a cheerful giver is the point. If giving currently feels like a levy, it may be worth giving less rather than resenting it."),

                scday("Not high-minded",
                      ["1 Timothy 6:17-19", "Acts 20:35"],
                      "Charge them that are rich that they be not high-minded, nor trust in uncertain riches. Uncertain is the operative word; the warning is against leaning on something that moves. The instruction that follows is to be ready to distribute. Paul then quotes a line about it being more blessed to give than to receive. Blessed here is closer to fortunate than to holy. It is an observation about where the good is."),

                scday("Never forsaken",
                      ["Hebrews 13:5-6", "Philippians 4:19"],
                      "Let your conversation be without covetousness. Conversation in the older sense means your whole manner of life, not your talking. The reason given is not that money will arrive but that presence will: I will never leave thee. Philippians speaks of need supplied, and need is the word used, not want. Nothing here guarantees comfort. It refuses to let scarcity be the final description of your situation."),
               ]),

        scplan("hope-hard-seasons",
               "Hope in Hard Seasons",
               "Three weeks of small light",
               .hope,
               "Three weeks for a stretch that has gone on longer than expected: illness, grief, waiting, or a season with no clear name. It does not rush the difficulty or explain it away. It stays close to the passages that were written by people in the middle of something.",
               [
                scday("Near the broken",
                      ["Psalms 34:18", "Psalms 147:3"],
                      "The Lord is nigh unto them that are of a broken heart. Nigh, not fixing, at least not in that sentence. The psalm places nearness before repair, and the order matters to anyone who has been offered a solution when they wanted company. He healeth the broken in heart and bindeth up their wounds. Binding is slow work, done in layers, and it is usually done sitting down."),

                scday("Not consumed",
                      ["Lamentations 3:22", "Lamentations 3:23"],
                      "It is his mercies that we are not consumed. The book is called Lamentations for a reason; nothing around this verse is going well. The claim is minimal and therefore believable: not thriving, not restored, simply not finished off. New every morning is the next line. Not new every hour. If today's supply is gone by four in the afternoon, that appears to be normal."),

                scday("Waiting quietly",
                      ["Lamentations 3:25", "Lamentations 3:26"],
                      "It is good that a man should both hope and quietly wait. Quietly is the hard word. Most waiting is noisy inside even when it looks still from outside. The verse does not describe waiting as a pause before real life resumes; it calls the waiting itself good, which is not the same as pleasant. What would it change to stop treating this season as time being wasted?"),

                scday("Through the water",
                      ["Isaiah 43:2", "Isaiah 41:10"],
                      "When thou passest through the waters, I will be with thee. Through, not around, and the fire is not extinguished either. This is not a promise of exemption, which is exactly why it is usable. Fear thou not, for I am with thee. If the only assurance offered is company, that may still be the difference between drowning and passing through."),

                scday("Renewed strength",
                      ["Isaiah 40:31", "Isaiah 40:28"],
                      "They shall mount up with wings as eagles, they shall run and not be weary, they shall walk and not faint. The sequence descends: flying, running, then walking. Walking without fainting is the last and the most useful. The chapter opens by insisting that the everlasting God does not faint or grow weary, which is offered to people who plainly do. Today, walking counts."),

                scday("Cast down",
                      ["Psalms 42:5", "Psalms 42:1"],
                      "Why art thou cast down, O my soul? The psalmist is arguing with himself, out loud, and losing intermittently. That alone is worth noticing: this is in the book, unresolved. As the hart panteth after the water brooks, so panteth my soul. Thirst is not a failure of faith. It is the accurate report of someone a long way from water."),

                scday("The valley",
                      ["Psalms 23:4", "Psalms 23:1"],
                      "Yea, though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death. The verb is walk, and the destination is through. Something in the psalm shifts here, from talking about a shepherd to talking to him: thou art with me. People often do that in the dark. If your praying has changed from description to direct address lately, that is not a decline."),

                scday("Morning",
                      ["Psalms 30:5", "Psalms 126:5"],
                      "Weeping may endure for a night, but joy cometh in the morning. Endure is a fair word for it; nights are long and this one may last months. The psalm does not say the night was short. Elsewhere: they that sow in tears shall reap in joy. The sowing is done weeping, which means the tears are not an interruption of the work. They are the conditions in which it happens."),

                scday("A present help",
                      ["Psalms 46:1", "Psalms 46:10"],
                      "A very present help in trouble. Present is doing the work in that line, and the trouble described in the psalm is not small: mountains moving, waters roaring. Later comes be still, and know. Be still is closer to let go, or stop striving, than to sit quietly. Some days the only available act is to stop pushing at something that will not move today."),

                scday("Casting the weight",
                      ["1 Peter 5:6-7", "Psalms 55:22"],
                      "Casting all your care upon him, for he careth for you. The same word turns twice: your anxious care, and his caring about you. The psalm has the same picture, cast thy burden. Neither says the burden disappears from the world. They say you were not designed to be its only carrier. What have you been holding this week that could be set down for one evening?"),

                scday("Not crushed",
                      ["2 Corinthians 4:7-9", "2 Corinthians 4:16-18"],
                      "Troubled on every side, yet not distressed; perplexed, but not in despair. Paul lists what is happening and then adds one small word each time. Not destroyed. It is the narrowest of margins and it is the whole claim. The outward man perisheth, he goes on, but the inward man is renewed day by day. Day by day, not once. Renewal on that schedule is easy to miss while it is happening."),

                scday("Patience and experience",
                      ["Romans 5:3-5", "James 1:2-4"],
                      "Tribulation worketh patience, and patience experience, and experience hope. It is a chain, and chains take time; nobody skips a link. James says something similar and asks for the patience to have its perfect work, meaning its complete work. Neither says the suffering is good. Both say something is being made. That is a slower comfort than being told it will end, and it holds up better."),

                scday("Nothing separates",
                      ["Romans 8:38-39", "Romans 8:35-37"],
                      "Neither death, nor life, nor angels, nor principalities, nor things present, nor things to come. Paul writes a list because lists are what anxious people make. He includes height and depth, then adds nor any other creature, in case he has missed one. The earlier verses name tribulation, famine, peril, sword, which were his actual circumstances. He is not speaking hypothetically."),

                scday("All things together",
                      ["Romans 8:28", "Genesis 50:20"],
                      "All things work together for good. The verse is often used to tell people their suffering was good, which it does not say. It says things work together, which is a claim about a whole rather than a rating of each part. Joseph tells his brothers that what they meant for evil was meant otherwise, without once calling what they did anything but evil. Both things stayed true."),

                scday("Groanings",
                      ["Romans 8:26-27", "Psalms 62:8"],
                      "We know not what we should pray for as we ought. That is a considerable admission to find in a letter about faith. The help offered is not better technique but groanings that cannot be uttered, prayer beneath the level of words. Pour out your heart before him, says the psalm. Pouring out is undignified and unedited. If you have run out of things to say, you are not outside the process."),

                scday("Though the fig tree",
                      ["Habakkuk 3:17-18", "Job 13:15"],
                      "Although the fig tree shall not blossom, neither shall fruit be in the vines. Habakkuk itemises the failure crop by crop, empty stall by empty stall, before he says yet will I rejoice. The yet is only possible because the list came first. Job says something harder still, that he will trust even if it costs him everything. Neither man is pretending. That is what makes them worth reading here."),

                scday("An anchor",
                      ["Hebrews 6:19", "Hebrews 12:1-2"],
                      "Hope as an anchor of the soul, both sure and stedfast. An anchor is not a sail. It does not move you anywhere; it keeps you from being carried off while the weather does what it does. Hebrews goes on to describe running with patience the race set before us, which assumes a course you did not choose. Some seasons the whole task is not drifting."),

                scday("A man of sorrows",
                      ["Isaiah 53:3", "Hebrews 4:14-16"],
                      "A man of sorrows, and acquainted with grief. Acquainted is a quiet word, suggesting long familiarity rather than a visit. Hebrews follows it with a priest touched with the feeling of our infirmities, and invites us to come boldly. Boldly, in the middle of weakness. Whatever else is uncertain, the text does not present suffering as something that has to be tidied up before you approach."),

                scday("Jesus wept",
                      ["John 11:35", "Psalms 56:3"],
                      "Jesus wept. The shortest verse, and it happens at a graveside where he already knows what he is about to do. The weeping is not made unnecessary by the outcome. That is worth sitting with if you have been told your grief means you have lost your grip. What time I am afraid, says the psalm, I will trust. What time, not if. The fear is assumed."),

                scday("Prisoners of hope",
                      ["Zechariah 9:12", "Romans 15:13"],
                      "Turn you to the strong hold, ye prisoners of hope. It is a strange phrase, hope as the thing holding you rather than the thing you hold. Some days that is the more accurate description; you are not being hopeful so much as unable to stop. Paul prays for filling with joy and peace in believing. In believing, not after it is all sorted out."),

                scday("All things new",
                      ["Revelation 21:3-4", "Revelation 21:5-7"],
                      "God shall wipe away all tears from their eyes. The gesture is close and personal, a hand at a face, not an announcement. And the former things are passed away. Behold, I make all things new. New, not replaced; the same things made new. Whatever this stretch has cost you, the last word in the book is not about erasing it but about repair. That is a slower promise, and a kinder one."),
               ]),

        scplan("rest-and-sabbath",
               "Rest",
               "One week of slowing down",
               .rest,
               "Seven short readings about rest: the kind built into the week, and the kind you have to defend. For anyone who is tired, or who suspects that being busy has stopped being a season and started being a personality.",
               [
                scday("He rested",
                      ["Genesis 2:2", "Exodus 20:8"],
                      "On the seventh day he rested. It is a startling detail in a story about limitless power, and it appears before any commandment about it exists. Later the day is set apart and the reason given is simply that this happened. Rest is presented as part of the design rather than a reward for finishing. You will not finish. That was never the condition."),

                scday("Come apart",
                      ["Mark 6:31", "Mark 1:35"],
                      "Come ye yourselves apart into a desert place, and rest a while. The reason given is delightfully mundane: there were so many coming and going that they had no leisure to eat. Elsewhere he rises a great while before day and goes out to a solitary place. Both scenes involve leaving people who still wanted something. Rest usually requires disappointing someone. That is the cost, and it is worth it."),

                scday("Green pastures",
                      ["Psalms 23:2", "Psalms 23:3"],
                      "He maketh me to lie down in green pastures. Maketh is the word that surprises people. Sheep do not lie down while anxious, so the shepherd's task is partly to make the field safe enough that rest becomes possible. He leadeth me beside the still waters. Restoring follows the lying down, not the other way round. What would make your evening safe enough to stop in?"),

                scday("An easy yoke",
                      ["Matthew 11:28-30", "Jeremiah 6:16"],
                      "Take my yoke upon you, for my yoke is easy and my burden is light. Easy in the older sense means well fitting, a yoke shaped so it does not chafe. This is not an offer of no work; it is an offer of work that suits you. Jeremiah says stand in the ways and ask for the old paths, and ye shall find rest for your souls. Both involve stopping first to look."),

                scday("One thing needful",
                      ["Luke 10:41-42", "Luke 5:16"],
                      "Thou art careful and troubled about many things. Careful in older English means full of care, weighed down, not conscientious. Martha is not scolded for working; she is told that many things have crowded out one. Luke elsewhere notes a habit of withdrawing into the wilderness to pray, stated as routine rather than event. Habits of withdrawal are rarely convenient. They are almost never regretted."),

                scday("In quietness",
                      ["Isaiah 30:15", "Psalms 46:10"],
                      "In returning and rest shall ye be saved; in quietness and in confidence shall be your strength. The verse ends with a sad half line: and ye would not. It was offered and declined, which is the fate of most rest. Be still, and know, says the psalm, where being still is closer to letting your hands drop. Some knowing is only available after the striving stops."),

                scday("My presence",
                      ["Exodus 33:14", "Psalms 62:5"],
                      "My presence shall go with thee, and I will give thee rest. Moses had asked for a guarantee before moving, and what he received was company rather than a route. Rest here is not the absence of a journey. My soul, wait thou only upon God, says the psalm, for my expectation is from him. Waiting only is difficult. It means putting down the second and third plans."),
               ]),

        scplan("honest-questions",
               "Honest Questions",
               "Ten days for real doubts",
               .doubt,
               "Ten days for a reader with questions that have not been answered. It does not try to talk anyone out of doubting, and it does not tidy the difficult passages. Doubt appears throughout the text itself, often in the mouths of people who stayed.",
               [
                scday("Why hast thou",
                      ["Psalms 22:1", "Job 14:1"],
                      "My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me? The sentence is in the book. Nobody edited it out, and it was later quoted by a dying man who presumably knew the psalm. Job adds that man born of woman is of few days and full of trouble, which reads like a complaint because it is one. If your prayers have turned into questions, you are using a form the text supplies."),

                scday("Help my unbelief",
                      ["Mark 9:23-24", "Mark 5:36"],
                      "Lord, I believe; help thou mine unbelief. It is a contradiction said out loud by a father with a sick child, and it is not corrected. The two halves stand together, which suggests belief and unbelief can occupy one person at once without either cancelling the other. Only believe, comes elsewhere, said to someone whose news had just got worse. Believing is not being certain. It is continuing to ask."),

                scday("Reach hither",
                      ["John 20:27", "John 20:29"],
                      "Reach hither thy finger, and behold my hands. Thomas had said he would not believe without evidence, and the response is to offer evidence. Whatever you make of the story, note that the demand was met rather than rebuked. The line about those who have not seen is a blessing on later readers, not an insult to him. Wanting something to examine is treated here as reasonable."),

                scday("Secret things",
                      ["Deuteronomy 29:29", "Isaiah 55:8-9"],
                      "The secret things belong unto God, but those things which are revealed belong unto us. That is a division of labour, and it concedes openly that a portion is not disclosed. Isaiah adds that the distance between his thoughts and ours is like the distance between sky and ground. Neither passage says stop asking. They say that some of what you are asking about was never on the page."),

                scday("Where wast thou",
                      ["Job 38:4", "Job 38:7"],
                      "Where wast thou when I laid the foundations of the earth? Job has spent chapters demanding an answer, and what arrives is a long series of questions about weather and wild animals and the morning stars singing. It is not an explanation, and the argument he made is never refuted. Something in the scale of the reply reaches him anyway. That is either deeply unsatisfying or the only honest kind of answer."),

                scday("Now mine eye",
                      ["Job 42:5", "Job 42:2"],
                      "I have heard of thee by the hearing of the ear, but now mine eye seeth thee. Job never learns why. The reader was told in chapter one; he was not, and the book ends without him being informed. What changes is the relationship rather than the information. If you have been waiting to resolve the question before you can proceed, notice that he proceeded without resolving it."),

                scday("Through a glass",
                      ["1 Corinthians 13:11-12", "1 Corinthians 2:9"],
                      "For now we see through a glass, darkly. The glass is a polished metal mirror, which gave a dim and warped reflection; the image is of poor sight, not of a window. Paul includes himself: I know in part. Elsewhere he quotes a line about things eye has not seen nor ear heard. Partial vision is described as the current condition of everyone, including the person writing the letter."),

                scday("A still small voice",
                      ["1 Kings 19:12", "1 Kings 18:21"],
                      "Not in the wind, not in the earthquake, not in the fire, but a still small voice. The Hebrew is closer to a sound of thin silence. Elijah had just seen fire fall in public and was now hiding in a cave wanting to die. Earlier he had asked a crowd how long they would halt between two opinions. He is now halting himself. The text lets both stand."),

                scday("One thing I know",
                      ["John 9:25", "John 8:31-32"],
                      "Whether he be a sinner or no, I know not: one thing I know, that whereas I was blind, now I see. The man being interrogated declines to argue theology and reports what happened to him. It is a small, defensible position. The truth shall make you free, says an earlier passage, addressed to people who are told to continue in it first. Continuing is presented as how you find out."),

                scday("A reason for hope",
                      ["1 Peter 3:15", "Jeremiah 29:13"],
                      "Be ready always to give an answer to every man that asketh you a reason of the hope that is in you. The word for answer is where we get apology, in its older sense of a defense. It assumes hope has reasons rather than being a mood. And Jeremiah: ye shall seek me and find me, when ye shall search for me with all your heart. Searching is not the opposite of finding."),
               ]),
    ]
}
